module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Modules.Tilde
public import Architect
public import Hironaka.Ch0.BlowingUpModules
public import Hironaka.Compat.ProjDegreeOne

/-!
# Ch. 0 §2 (pp. 124 and 128): `Proj` of the symmetric algebra and of the Rees algebra

Grothendieck's observation (cited, footnote 13) and the claims of p. 128 about `Proj` of the Rees algebra.  The
statements are `def … : Prop` and are not proved here; the objects they speak about are built in
`Hironaka/Compat/ProjDegreeOne.lean`, `Hironaka/Compat/SymmetricAlgebraGrading.lean` and
`Hironaka/Compat/ReesAlgebraGrading.lean`.

> The proofs of the basic existence theorem of blowing-up and of the assertions of (a) and (b) are based on the
> following observation of A. Grothendieck:¹³ Let A be a commutative ring with unity, and J an A-module. Let
> S_A(J) denote the symmetric tensor algebra over A, i.e., the graded A-algebra whose homogeneous part of degree n,
> S_A(J)_n, is the nᵗʰ symmetric tensor product of J with itself over A. In particular, S_A(J)₀ ≈ A and
> S_A(J)₁ ≈ J. Then we construct the A-scheme Proj(S_A(J)) = (X, O_X) where X is the set of those homogeneous prime
> ideals in S_A(J) which do not contain S_A(J)₁ and, if Spec(S_A(J)) = (X′, O_X′), O_X is the subsheaf of rings of
> the restriction O_X′ | X which consists of homogeneous elements of degree zero. Let J₁ denote the sheaf of modules
> on Proj(S_A(J)) obtained as the subsheaf of modules of O_X′ | X which consists of homogeneous elements of degree
> one. We have then a canonical homomorphism of the module S_A(J)₁ (≈J) into Γ(X, J₁) such that the image of
> S_A(J)₁ generates the sheaf J₁ of O_X-modules. Thus, if we denote by J the sheaf of modules on Spec(A) generated
> by J, we obtain a canonical morphism Φ of ℛ-modules from (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁) to (Spec(A), J). Due to A. Grothendieck
> is the fact that the pair of the ℛ-module (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁) and Φ: (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁) → (Spec(A), J) is a
> blowing-up of (Spec(A), J) in the category 𝓜(ℛ). (p. 124; footnote 13: "cf. § 2, Ch. II, of Grothendieck [6]")

> Let S*_A(J) denote the graded A-algebra obtained as the direct sum of Jⁿ (the nᵗʰ power of J in A) for all
> non-negative integers n. Then we have a canonical morphism of Proj(S*_A(J)) to Spec(A). Let I₁* denote the sheaf
> of modules on Proj(S*_A(J)) generated by the homogeneous part of degree one of S*_A(J). Then we have an obvious
> homomorphism of I₁* to the sheaf of ideal on Proj(S*_A(J)) generated by J. We can prove that this homomorphism is
> bijective. By means of this isomorphism, we view I₁* as a sheaf of ideals on Proj(S*_A(J)). We then have a natural
> morphism of ℛ-ideals from (Proj(S*_A(J)), I₁*) to S(A, J). We can prove that (Proj(S*_A(J), I₁*)) [sic] with this
> morphism is a blowing-up of S(A, J) in the category 𝒥(ℛ). (p. 128)

## What is formalized

* The objects: `S(A, J) = (Spec A, J~)` and `P(A, J) = (Proj S_A(J), J₁)` as `ℛ`-modules (`symmetricAffine`,
  `symmetricProj`); `S(A, J)` for an ideal as an `ℛ`-ideal (`specIdeal`); the sheaf `I₁*` on `Proj S*_A(J)`
  (`ProjDegreeOne.degreeOneSheaf` for the grading `ProjDegreeOne.reesGrading`); the `ℛ`-ideal
  `(Proj S*_A(J), I₁*)` and the morphism to `S(A, J)` from an isomorphism `I₁* ≅ f⁻¹(J)` (`reesIdealObject`,
  `reesIdealMorphism`).
* The claims, each as a `def … : Prop` not proved here: the generation of `J₁` by `S_A(J)₁`
  (`SymmetricDegreeOneGenerates`), Grothendieck's observation (`GrothendieckObservation`), the bijectivity of
  the obvious homomorphism for the Rees algebra (`ReesDegreeOneIsIdeal`), and the blowing-up property
  (`ReesProjIsBlowingUp`).

## Formalization notes (encodings)

* `Proj` is Mathlib's `Proj` (homogeneous localizations), not the printed "subsheaf of `O_X′ | X` of homogeneous elements
  of degree zero" on the set of homogeneous primes not containing `S₁`; the two agree (the irrelevant ideal is
  generated by `S₁` for the symmetric and the Rees algebra), and this is not proved here.  The sheaf `J₁` is built in the same way,
  as the fractions `a / s` with `a` of degree `i + 1` and `s` of degree `i` (`ProjDegreeOne`).  The grading of
  `S_A(J)` is by the powers of the range of `ι` (`S_A(J)₁ ≈ J` is the paper's remark, not used), that of
  `S*_A(J)` is the Rees algebra of Mathlib (`reesAlgebra`, `⊕ Jⁿ Xⁿ`) with its pieces `Jⁿ Xⁿ`.
* The canonical morphism of `Proj` to `Spec A` is the morphism corresponding to `A → Γ(Proj, O)`, `a ↦ a / 1`, under the
  adjunction `Γ ⊣ Spec` (it is Mathlib's `Proj.toSpecZero` followed by `Spec` of `A → 𝒜₀`; not proved here).
* The paper's `J` (the sheaf generated by the module) is `tilde`.  For an ideal `J` of `A` the paper's `S(A, J)` has
  the sheaf of ideals generated by `J`; it is encoded as the image of `J~ → O` (`specIdeal`), which is `J~`
  (not proved here); for the module `J` of the observation the sheaf is `J~` itself.
* "Generates" for the canonical homomorphism `f^*(J~) → J₁` is that it is an epimorphism (`Epi`), as in the definition
  of a morphism of `ℛ`-modules; the printed sentence states the generation inside the construction of `Φ`, so the
  observation is stated as the existence of this epimorphism property together with the blowing-up property.
* The "obvious homomorphism" `I₁* → f⁻¹(J)` is characterized by compatibility with the canonical maps from `f^*(J~)`:
  `reesCanonical ≫ α.hom = reesPullbackCanonical`.  The claim of bijectivity is the existence of an isomorphism `α`
  with this property (`ReesDegreeOneIsIdeal`); the blowing-up claim is for every such `α`
  (`ReesProjIsBlowingUp`).  The paper does not define the obvious homomorphism beyond "obvious".
* The blowing-up in `𝒥(ℛ)` is the universal property (**) with respect to the functor `RIdeal.toRModuleFunctor`,
  as in `IdealsNotClosedUnderBlowingUp`.

## Not formalized

* The construction of the blowing-up `(N, Φ)` of an arbitrary `ℛ`-module on pp. 124–125 is not here: it is
  `IsConstruction` in `Hironaka/Ch0/BlowingUpConstruction.lean`, with its claims `ConstructionIsRModule` and
  `ConstructionIsBlowingUp` (stated, not proved).
* The remark at the start of the second paragraph of p. 128 (the observation applied to the module `J`, for the
  sheaf `I₁` generated by `S_A(J)₁`): it follows from `GrothendieckObservation` and `J~ ≅ J`, and is not restated.

## Misprints of the source

p. 128: the parenthesis in "(Proj(S*_A(J), I₁*))" is misplaced (quoted with [sic]); "sheaf of ideal" for "sheaf of ideals".
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory AlgebraicGeometry

noncomputable section

namespace Hironaka

universe u

open ProjDegreeOne

section Symmetric

variable (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (J : Type u) [AddCommGroup J] [Module A J]

/-- `S(A, J) = (Spec A, J~)` for an `A`-module `J`, as an `ℛ`-module.

> Thus, if we denote by J the sheaf of modules on Spec(A) generated by J … (p. 124)
> Let us write S(A, J) for (Spec(A), J) and P(A, J) for (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁). (p. 124)

Formalization note (encoding): the sheaf of modules generated by `J` is Mathlib's `tilde`. -/
def symmetricAffine : RModule.{u} := ⟨(Spec A).toLocallyRingedSpace, tildeOM J⟩

/-- `P(A, J) = (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁)`, as an `ℛ`-module.

> Let us write S(A, J) for (Spec(A), J) and P(A, J) for (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁). (p. 124)

Definition (p. 124).  Formalization note (encoding): `Proj` is Mathlib's, the grading of the symmetric algebra
is by the powers of the range of `ι`, and `J₁` is `ProjDegreeOne.degreeOneSheaf`; see the module docstring. -/
@[blueprint "def:0.S2-symmetric-proj"
  (statement := /-- Let $S_A(J)$ denote the symmetric tensor algebra over $A$, i.e., the graded $A$-algebra whose homogeneous
    part of degree $n$ is the $n$-th symmetric tensor product of $J$ with itself over $A$. Then we construct the
    $A$-scheme $\operatorname{Proj}(S_A(J))$, and let $J_1$ denote the sheaf of modules on it consisting of homogeneous
    elements of degree one. Let us write $S(A,J)$ for $(\operatorname{Spec}(A),\tilde J)$ and $P(A,J)$ for
    $(\operatorname{Proj}(S_A(J)),J_1)$. (p.~124.) Formalization note (encoding): $\operatorname{Proj}$ is Mathlib's
    (homogeneous localizations), $J_1$ is the sheaf of fractions $a/s$ with $a$ of degree $i+1$ and $s$ of degree $i$,
    and $\tilde J$ is Mathlib's $\widetilde J$. -/)
  (latexEnv := "definition")]
def symmetricProj : RModule.{u} :=
  ⟨(Proj (symmetricGrading A J)).toLocallyRingedSpace, degreeOneSheaf (symmetricGrading A J)⟩

/-- The canonical homomorphism `f^*(J~) → J₁` is an epimorphism: "the image of `S_A(J)₁` generates the sheaf `J₁`".

> We have then a canonical homomorphism of the module S_A(J)₁ (≈J) into Γ(X, J₁) such that the image of S_A(J)₁
> generates the sheaf J₁ of O_X-modules. (p. 124)

Cited with Grothendieck's observation (footnote 13, [6] Ch. II §2); not proved here.  Formalization note
(encoding): the homomorphism is the adjoint of `J → Γ(Proj, J₁)`, `j ↦ ι(j) / 1`, and "generates" is `Epi`
(surjective on stalks). -/
@[blueprint "prop:0.S2-symmetric-generation"
  (statement := /-- We have then a canonical homomorphism of the module $S_A(J)_1$ into $\Gamma(X,J_1)$ such that the image of
    $S_A(J)_1$ generates the sheaf $J_1$ of $O_X$-modules. (p.~124.) Formalization note (encoding): ``generates'' is that
    the induced homomorphism $f^*(\tilde J)\to J_1$ is an epimorphism. Not proved here: cited (footnote 13). -/)
  (latexEnv := "proposition")]
def SymmetricDegreeOneGenerates : Prop :=
  ∀ (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (J : Type u) [AddCommGroup J] [Module A J],
    Epi (symmetricCanonical A J)

/-- The canonical morphism `Φ` of `ℛ`-modules from `(Proj(S_A(J)), J₁)` to `(Spec(A), J~)` (p. 124), given
the epimorphism property of its sheaf component.

Definition (p. 124). -/
def symmetricMorphism (h : Epi (symmetricCanonical A J)) : symmetricProj A J ⟶ symmetricAffine A J :=
  ⟨structureLRS (symmetricGrading A J), symmetricCanonical A J, h⟩

/-- Grothendieck's observation: `(Proj(S_A(J)), J₁)` with `Φ` is a blowing-up of `(Spec(A), J~)` in `𝓜(ℛ)`.

> Due to A. Grothendieck is the fact that the pair of the ℛ-module (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁) and
> Φ: (Proj(S_A(J)), J₁) → (Spec(A), J) is a blowing-up of (Spec(A), J) in the category 𝓜(ℛ). (p. 124)

Cited (Grothendieck [6], Ch. II §2; footnote 13, p. 124); not proved here.  Formalization note (encoding): the
canonical morphism `Φ` exists, namely it is an epimorphism on sheaves (the generation of `J₁`, printed in the
same passage), and it is a blowing-up in `𝓜(ℛ)` (the universal property (**) with `T` the identity functor). -/
@[blueprint "thm:0.S2-grothendieck-observation"
  (statement := /-- \textbf{(A.~Grothendieck)} Let $A$ be a commutative ring with unity and $J$ an $A$-module. The pair of the
    $\mathcal R$-module $(\operatorname{Proj}(S_A(J)),J_1)$ and $\Phi\colon(\operatorname{Proj}(S_A(J)),J_1)\to
    (\operatorname{Spec}(A),\tilde J)$ is a blowing-up of $(\operatorname{Spec}(A),\tilde J)$ in the category
    $\mathcal M(\mathcal R)$. (p.~124; footnote 13: cf.\ \S2, Ch.~II, of Grothendieck [6].) Not proved here: cited. -/)
  (latexEnv := "theorem")]
def GrothendieckObservation : Prop :=
  ∀ (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (J : Type u) [AddCommGroup J] [Module A J],
    ∃ h : Epi (symmetricCanonical A J),
      IsBlowingUp (𝟭 RModule.{u}) (symmetricMorphism A J h)

end Symmetric

section PullbackIdeal

variable {X' : LocallyRingedSpace.{u}} (I : RIdeal.{u}) (f : X' ⟶ I.X)

/-- The sheaf of ideals `f⁻¹(J)` of `X'` (the sheaf of `pullbackIdeal`), typed over `X'`. -/
abbrev RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ : OModules.{u} X' := (I.pullbackIdeal f).J

/-- The inclusion `f⁻¹(J) ⟶ O_{X'}`. -/
abbrev RIdeal.pullbackIdealι : RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f ⟶ OModules.unit X' := (I.pullbackIdeal f).ι

instance : Mono (RIdeal.pullbackIdealι I f) := RIdeal.mono_ι _

/-- The canonical `f`-epimorphism `f^*(J) ⟶ f⁻¹(J)` of `ℛ`-ideals (p. 128, "`φ` of `J₂` to `θ(J₂)O_{X₁}`"). -/
def RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal : (OModules.pullback f).obj I.J ⟶ RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f :=
  Abelian.factorThruImage _

theorem RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal_ι :
    RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal I f ≫ RIdeal.pullbackIdealι I f =
      (OModules.pullback f).map I.ι ≫ OModules.pullbackUnitToUnit f :=
  Abelian.image.fac _

theorem RIdeal.epi_toPullbackIdeal_comp_inv {R : OModules.{u} X'} (α : R ≅ RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f) :
    Epi (RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal I f ≫ α.inv) := by
  have : Epi (RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal I f) := inferInstanceAs (Epi (Abelian.factorThruImage _))
  have : IsIso α.inv := inferInstance
  infer_instance

theorem RIdeal.compat_toPullbackIdeal {R : OModules.{u} X'} (α : R ≅ RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f) :
    (OModules.pullback f).map I.ι ≫ OModules.pullbackUnitToUnit f =
      (RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal I f ≫ α.inv) ≫ α.hom ≫ RIdeal.pullbackIdealι I f := by
  rw [Category.assoc, Iso.inv_hom_id_assoc, RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal_ι]

theorem RIdeal.mono_iso_comp_ι {R : OModules.{u} X'} (α : R ≅ RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f) :
    Mono (α.hom ≫ RIdeal.pullbackIdealι I f) := by
  have : IsIso α.hom := inferInstance
  infer_instance

/-- The `ℛ`-ideal `(X', R)` obtained from an isomorphism `R ≅ f⁻¹(J)` ("by means of this isomorphism, we view
`R` as a sheaf of ideals", p. 128). -/
def RIdeal.ofIso {R : OModules.{u} X'} (α : R ≅ RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f) : RIdeal.{u} :=
  RIdeal.mk X' R (α.hom ≫ RIdeal.pullbackIdealι I f) (RIdeal.mono_iso_comp_ι I f α)

/-- The morphism of `ℛ`-ideals from `(X', R)` to `(X, J)`, given by `f` and the canonical `f`-epimorphism of `J` to
`f⁻¹(J)` (p. 128: "a natural morphism of `ℛ`-ideals"). -/
def RIdeal.ofIsoHom {R : OModules.{u} X'} (α : R ≅ RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ I f) :
    RIdeal.ofIso I f α ⟶ I :=
  ⟨⟨f, RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal I f ≫ α.inv, RIdeal.epi_toPullbackIdeal_comp_inv I f α⟩,
    RIdeal.compat_toPullbackIdeal I f α⟩

end PullbackIdeal

section Rees

variable (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (J : Ideal A)

/-- The inclusion `J~ → O_{Spec A}` of an ideal (the tilde of `J → A`). -/
def idealIncl : tildeOM J ⟶ OModules.unit (Spec A).toLocallyRingedSpace :=
  (tilde.functor A).map (ModuleCat.ofHom J.subtype) ≫ tildeSelf.hom

/-- `S(A, J) = (Spec A, J)` for an ideal `J` of `A`, with the sheaf of ideals `J` on `Spec A` generated by `J`, as an
`ℛ`-ideal.

> Let A be a commutative ring with unity, and J an ideal in A. Then S(A, J) (= (X, J) with X = Spec(A) and with the
> sheaf of ideals J on X generated by J) can be viewed both as an ℛ-module and as an ℛ-ideal, i.e., an
> (ℛ/Z)-ideal. (p. 128)

Definition (p. 128).  Formalization note (encoding): the sheaf of ideals generated by `J` is the image of `J~ → O`
(its monomorphism into `O` is the image inclusion). -/
@[blueprint "def:0.S2-spec-ideal"
  (statement := /-- Let $A$ be a commutative ring with unity, and $J$ an ideal in $A$. Then $S(A,J)$ ($=(X,J)$ with
    $X=\operatorname{Spec}(A)$ and with the sheaf of ideals $J$ on $X$ generated by $J$) can be viewed both as an
    $\mathcal R$-module and as an $\mathcal R$-ideal, i.e., an $(\mathcal R/\mathbb Z)$-ideal. (p.~128.) Formalization
    note (encoding): the sheaf of ideals generated by $J$ is the image of $\tilde J\to O_{\operatorname{Spec}A}$. -/)
  (latexEnv := "definition")]
def specIdeal : RIdeal.{u} :=
  ⟨(Spec A).toLocallyRingedSpace, Abelian.image (idealIncl A J), Abelian.image.ι _, inferInstance⟩

/-- The sheaf of ideals `J · O` on `Proj(S*_A(J))` generated by `J`: `f⁻¹(J)` for the canonical morphism `f`
(p. 128: "the sheaf of ideal on Proj(S*_A(J)) generated by J"; the notation `f⁻¹(J₂)` of p. 128). -/
abbrev reesGeneratedIdeal : OModules.{u} (Proj (reesGrading A J)).toLocallyRingedSpace :=
  RIdeal.pullbackIdealJ (specIdeal A J) (structureLRS (reesGrading A J))

/-- The canonical homomorphism `f^*(J~) → J · O` (a composite of epimorphisms): the one with which the "obvious
homomorphism" of `I₁*` to `J · O` (p. 128) is compatible.

Formalization note (encoding): the paper only says "obvious"; see `ReesDegreeOneIsIdeal`. -/
def reesPullbackCanonical :
    (OModules.pullback (structureLRS (reesGrading A J))).obj (tildeOM J) ⟶ reesGeneratedIdeal A J :=
  (OModules.pullback (structureLRS (reesGrading A J))).map
      (Abelian.factorThruImage (idealIncl A J)) ≫
    RIdeal.toPullbackIdeal (specIdeal A J) (structureLRS (reesGrading A J))

/-- The obvious homomorphism of `I₁*` to the sheaf of ideals generated by `J` is bijective.

> Let I₁* denote the sheaf of modules on Proj(S*_A(J)) generated by the homogeneous part of degree one of S*_A(J).
> Then we have an obvious homomorphism of I₁* to the sheaf of ideal on Proj(S*_A(J)) generated by J. We can prove
> that this homomorphism is bijective. (p. 128)

Stated as provable without a proof being given (p. 128, "We can prove", deferred by footnote 2, p. 114); not
proved here.  Formalization note (encoding): the obvious homomorphism is characterized by its compatibility with the
canonical maps from `f^*(J~)` (`reesCanonical`, `reesPullbackCanonical`); the claim is the existence of an
isomorphism with this property. -/
@[blueprint "prop:0.S2-rees-ideal-iso"
  (statement := /-- Let $S^*_A(J)$ denote the graded $A$-algebra obtained as the direct sum of $J^n$ for all non-negative
    integers $n$, and $I_1^*$ the sheaf of modules on $\operatorname{Proj}(S^*_A(J))$ generated by the homogeneous part
    of degree one. Then we have an obvious homomorphism of $I_1^*$ to the sheaf of ideals on
    $\operatorname{Proj}(S^*_A(J))$ generated by $J$. We can prove that this homomorphism is bijective. (p.~128.)
    Formalization note (encoding): the obvious homomorphism is the one compatible with the canonical maps from
    $f^*(\tilde J)$; the claim is the existence of such an isomorphism. Not proved here: stated as provable, no
    proof given (deferred, fn.~2). -/)
  (latexEnv := "proposition")]
def ReesDegreeOneIsIdeal : Prop :=
  ∀ (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (J : Ideal A),
    ∃ α : degreeOneSheaf (reesGrading A J) ≅ reesGeneratedIdeal A J,
      reesCanonical A J ≫ α.hom = reesPullbackCanonical A J

variable {A J}

/-- The `ℛ`-ideal `(Proj(S*_A(J)), I₁*)`, `I₁*` viewed as a sheaf of ideals by means of the isomorphism `α`
(p. 128: "By means of this isomorphism, we view I₁* as a sheaf of ideals on Proj(S*_A(J))"). -/
abbrev reesIdealObject (α : degreeOneSheaf (reesGrading A J) ≅ reesGeneratedIdeal A J) : RIdeal.{u} :=
  RIdeal.ofIso (specIdeal A J) (structureLRS (reesGrading A J)) α

/-- The natural morphism of `ℛ`-ideals from `(Proj(S*_A(J)), I₁*)` to `S(A, J)` (p. 128: "We then have a natural morphism of
`ℛ`-ideals from (Proj(S*_A(J)), I₁*) to S(A, J)"). -/
abbrev reesIdealMorphism (α : degreeOneSheaf (reesGrading A J) ≅ reesGeneratedIdeal A J) :
    reesIdealObject α ⟶ specIdeal A J :=
  RIdeal.ofIsoHom (specIdeal A J) (structureLRS (reesGrading A J)) α

/-- `(Proj(S*_A(J)), I₁*)` with the natural morphism is a blowing-up of `S(A, J)` in `𝒥(ℛ)`.

> We can prove that (Proj(S*_A(J), I₁*)) with this morphism is a blowing-up of S(A, J) in the category 𝒥(ℛ). (p. 128)

Stated as provable without a proof being given (p. 128, "We can prove", deferred by footnote 2, p. 114); not
proved here.  Formalization note (encoding): for every ring `A`, ideal `J` and isomorphism `α` as in `ReesDegreeOneIsIdeal`; the
blowing-up is in the sense of (**) with respect to the functor from `𝒥(ℛ)` to `𝓜(ℛ)`. -/
@[blueprint "thm:0.S2-rees-blowing-up"
  (statement := /-- We can prove that $(\operatorname{Proj}(S^*_A(J)),I_1^*)$ with this morphism is a blowing-up of $S(A,J)$ in
    the category $\mathcal J(\mathcal R)$. (p.~128; the printed parenthesis is misplaced.) Not proved here: stated as
    provable, no proof given (deferred, fn.~2). -/)
  (latexEnv := "theorem")]
def ReesProjIsBlowingUp : Prop :=
  ∀ (A : CommRingCat.{u}) (J : Ideal A) (α : degreeOneSheaf (reesGrading A J) ≅ reesGeneratedIdeal A J),
    reesCanonical A J ≫ α.hom = reesPullbackCanonical A J →
      IsBlowingUp RIdeal.toRModuleFunctor.{u} (reesIdealMorphism α)

end Rees

end Hironaka

end
